---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Principles | Amitoj Singh" description="Five personal distinctions about decisions, knowledge, autonomy, and a personal Sikh mapping for knowledge creation.">
  <!-- Hero Photo -->
  <section class="container-zen pt-12 pb-8">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <img
        src="/images/photography-optimized/DSCF0807.webp"
        alt="Tennis court in autumn"
        class="w-full h-72 object-cover"
        style="object-position: center center;"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen pt-8">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h1 class="mb-8 text-balance">Principles</h1>

      <div class="accent-line mb-8"></div>

      <p class="text-lg text-ink-light mb-6">
        I think clearly when I separate three things: <strong>what matters</strong>, <strong>what's true</strong>, and <strong>what I'm willing to do about it</strong>. I think most of my personal and professional friction comes from mixing up these questions and overestimating how clear the answers are.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <div class="space-y-12">

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">First: Values set direction. Evidence sets method.</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            "Should we?" is an evaluative question. It can't be solved by more data.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            "Can we?" is an epistemic question. It <em>can</em> be solved&mdash;slowly, imperfectly&mdash;by experiments, measurement, and iteration.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">So I try to route decisions accordingly:</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-ink-light space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Route "should" upward</strong> (to values, identity, responsibility).</li>
            <li><strong>Route "can" downward</strong> (to practice, prototypes, tests, and feedback).</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">From there: Capabilities are discovered, not declared.</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            I don't trust self-images&mdash;mine or anyone else's.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            "I can do X" is a <strong>scientific claim</strong>: it becomes real through repeated attempts under stated conditions, with honest accounting of failures.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            Constraints matter (failure teaches), but I try to orient around <strong>capability creation</strong>: expanding what I can reliably do, not just shrinking what I avoid.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">Then: Codify what works.</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            When something is repeatedly true, I compress it:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-ink-light mb-4 space-y-2">
            <li>into a <strong>principle</strong> when it requires judgment,</li>
            <li>into a <strong>system</strong> when it can be made deterministic.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            This is how knowledge becomes leverage: fewer decisions require fresh willpower every time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">But always: Protect what matters from what's measurable.</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            Metrics are useful, but they're not neutral.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            What's visible tends to become "real," and what's "real" tends to start running the show. I try to keep slow, non-measurable commitments (care, integrity, meaning) from getting colonized by fast proxies.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">Therefore: Earn autonomy. Don't gamble for it.</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            More freedom should come from more validated capability and tighter feedback loops&mdash;not from pretending risk is virtue.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl mb-8 section-header">A personal Sikh mapping</h2>

      <p class="text-ink-light mb-8">
        I use three Sikh practices as a personal sequence for knowledge creation: Naam Japna as epistemic humility, Kirat Karna as the work of testing, and Vandh Chakna as sharing what others can test and extend. This is my mapping, not a definition of the tradition.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl mb-4 text-sage-700">Naam Japna &mdash; epistemic humility</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            In this mapping, Naam Japna begins with the possibility that my model is wrong. Reality gets a vote, so the practice is to look for disconfirming evidence and update without drama.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl mb-4 text-sage-700">Kirat Karna &mdash; the work of testing</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            In this mapping, Kirat Karna is the work of testing. Choose a method, state the conditions, run the trial, and account for the result.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl mb-4 text-sage-700">Vandh Chakna &mdash; sharing what others can test and extend</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            In this mapping, Vandh Chakna moves the result outward. Publish the artifact, teach the method, and share the credit so others can test and extend the claim.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300 bg-oat-100">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">Continue</h2>

      <p class="text-ink-light mb-8">
        Research and writing show where these distinctions enter the rest of the work.
      </p>

      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
        <a href="/research" class="arrow-link">Research</a>
        <a href="/writing" class="arrow-link">Writing</a>
        <a href="/about" class="arrow-link">About</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
